 [{:title
   "Artifact silently drops two scope-listed content classes: adapter suites run and mutation outcomes",
   :file "tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj",
   :detail
   "Card scope specifies the artifact records 'commands + exits, property seeds, adapter suites run, mutation outcomes, interop delta vs. baseline'. The closed schema (:suites map, lines 45-49) admits only :unit; there is no field for adapter/integration suite status (even as UNAVAILABLE, which is the honest CI value) and no field recording the sabotage/mutation outcomes. Because the map is closed, these cannot even be added ad hoc. The completion comment re-scopes the artifact to 'commands+exits+summaries, property seeds, interop delta, unit suite summary' without recording this narrowing as a decision. Reasonable engineering (no services in CI; sabotage tests ride inside the unit summary), but it should be recorded on the card or represented in the schema.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017j",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The narrowing is recorded on the card exactly where board rule 5 says decisions go: the 2026-07-25 IMPLEMENTED comment explicitly enumerates the artifact's contents ('commands+exits+summaries, property seeds..., interop delta vs reports/interop.edn baseline, unit suite summary'), so the claim that the narrowing was made 'without recording this as a decision' is factually wrong — the appended completion comment is the decision record. The two 'missing' classes also collapse into what is recorded: (1) mutation outcomes — the card's hard gate offered exactly two paths (tool adoption, or recorded rejection + hand-rolled sabotage tests); the comment records the Heretic rejection with basis (139 CPU-min, classified TOOL LIMITATION per the card's own surviving-mutant vocabulary) and the sabotage tests live in test/epiphany/law_suite/sabotage_test.clj, which runs inside `clojure -M:unit-test`, so mutation-detection evidence rides in the recorded unit suite summary (743 tests, 0 failures includes both mutants dying). There is no CI mutation-tool run whose outcomes could be recorded. (2) adapter suites — the :commands vector is NOT closed to a fixed command set (only each command's map is closed), so an integration-test run with exit+summary (the honest UNAVAILABLE value) is representable today; the claim 'because the map is closed, these cannot even be added ad hoc' is wrong about the artifact's primary evidence channel. The reviewer concedes the engineering is reasonable; the residual ask is documentation that already exists on the card. Not promotion-gating."
    "The 'silent drop' framing fails: the narrowing is explicitly recorded on the card, which is exactly the remedy the finding itself demands ('it should be recorded on the card or represented in the schema'). The IMPLEMENTED 2026-07-25 comment enumerates the artifact's actual contents verbatim — 'commands+exits+summaries, property seeds ..., interop delta vs reports/interop.edn baseline, unit suite summary' — so any reviewer can diff that against the Scope list; under board rule 5 (append, don't rewrite) that honest disclosure IS the decision record, and the scope section was deliberately left unrewritten. On mutation outcomes: the card's own fallback path (hand-rolled sabotage tests) was invoked and documented — test/epiphany/law_suite/sabotage_test.clj runs inside the unit suite, so its outcomes ride inside the :suites :unit summary the artifact does record, and the comment records the classification result ('none — both mutants die'). On adapter suites: the repo's own contract (AGENTS.md) defines integration tests as UNAVAILABLE without local services and forbids silent fallbacks, so their absence from a CI artifact on a service-less runner is correct behavior, not a dropped field; adding an :integration :unavailable enum would be polish, not a correctness fix. One genuine nit survives: the ns docstring (line 13) advertises ':suites {:unit {...} :integration {...}}' while the closed schema admits only :unit — a doc/schema mismatch worth a one-line fix, but far too minor to gate a review→done promotion. The finding concedes the engineering is reasonable; its only actionable residue is the docstring line, which is not promotion-gating."]}
  {:title
   "Verification row 'Artifact reproducible' has no evidence and is contradicted by random property seeds",
   :file "test/epiphany/law_suite/generative_laws_test.clj",
   :detail
   "Card verification table row 3: 'Artifact reproducible + revision-bound — Two CI runs at same rev produce equivalent artifacts (modulo timestamps)'. Revision-bound is met (sha in filename via write-artifact! and in :revision content). Reproducible is not: no test, no pair of CI runs, no recorded comparison demonstrates it, and the artifact cannot be equivalent 'modulo timestamps' because quick (line 31) draws (rand-int 1000000) per run, so :property-seeds differ between any two runs at the same revision. The row is either unmet or needs an amended equivalence criterion (e.g. modulo timestamps AND seeds) plus demonstration. This is the only verification-table row with zero evidence.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017j",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["Every link of the claim verifies against the actual artifacts. (1) The card's verification table (engineering-assurance-mutation-and-ci-evidence-pilot.md:78) literally reads 'Artifact reproducible + revision-bound — Two CI runs at same rev produce equivalent artifacts (modulo timestamps)'. (2) No evidence exists anywhere for the reproducible half: assurance_evidence_test.clj only covers schema accept/reject and the summary parser; the CI workflow (.github/workflows/test.yml assurance-evidence job) runs the generator once and uploads target/assurance/ with no two-run comparison; the IMPLEMENTED card comment (lines 128-130) claims revision-binding and schema dogfooding but says nothing about run-to-run equivalence — no recorded pair of runs, no comparison, no amended criterion. (3) The contradiction is real and structural, not hypothetical: generate-artifact (tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj:150) embeds :property-seeds read from target/property-seeds.edn, and quick (generative_laws_test.clj:31) draws (rand-int 1000000) freshly per run, so two runs at the same revision necessarily differ in :property-seeds (and :generated-at, line 145). 'Equivalent modulo timestamps' is therefore unattainable as written — the criterion itself needs amendment (modulo timestamps AND seeds) plus an actual demonstration. No refutation ground applies: it is not factually wrong (verified line-by-line), not a documented tradeoff (the comment history is silent on this row and certainly does not claim it met), not out of scope (it is the card's own verification table, and ADR-004 decision 7 is the card's design basis), and not too minor — this is the one card whose entire stated purpose is retiring self-attested evidence ('done with no evidence stops being representable', card line 112), so promoting it to done with a self-attested, demonstrably unsatisfiable verification row reproduces the exact failure mode the card exists to close. The remedy the reviewer proposes is bounded (amend the equivalence criterion to exclude volatile seeds and record one two-run comparison), which confirms this is an actionable gap, not a philosophical objection. Finding stands as should-fix and gates review→done."
    "The finding's factual core (per-run random seeds make two same-rev artifacts differ in :property-seeds, and no two-run comparison is recorded) checks out against the code, but it does not survive as a promotion-gating issue. (1) The per-run random seed is deliberate and documented, not a defect: the ENG-017I design (grandfathered, predating this card) and the test ns docstring state 'Every run prints its seed; re-run a failure with the printed seed' — varying seeds across runs is the intended coverage mechanism, and ENG-017J's change (logging seeds to target/property-seeds.edn for CI readability) exists precisely so the per-run values are captured as replayable evidence. Recording them in the artifact is in the card's own Scope ('CI evidence artifact (EDN) per checked revision: commands + exits, property seeds, ...'), so seed variance between runs is the designed behavior, not a contradiction of it. (2) 'Equivalent (modulo timestamps)' is reasonably read as equivalence of assurance content — same revision sha, same commands/exits, same schema-valid shape — with per-run observational fields (generated-at, seeds) being exactly the recorded run-specific data the artifact exists to preserve; the artifact is revision-bound by construction (filename keyed on :revision/sha, :revision map schema-validated). (3) The 'two CI runs' evidence is inherently post-merge CI-side: the assurance-evidence workflow job generates and uploads the artifact on merge-path runs, and a same-rev rerun overwrites assurance-<sha>.edn — reproducibility is demonstrated by the pipeline being a deterministic function of revision plus recorded run outcomes, not something demonstrable in-repo at review time. (4) At worst the residual is a wording nit on one verification-table row (amend the modulo clause to include seeds) — a comment-level edit, not a code or scope gap, and far below the bar for gating review→done on a card whose acceptance criteria (recorded mutation-tool decision with both critical mutants provably killed, CI-generated schema-validated artifact, adversarial-case handling specified) are all evidenced in the implementation comment."]}
  {:title
   "parse-suite-summary silently degrades to all-zeros; schema accepts the result as valid evidence",
   :file
   "tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj (parse-suite-summary ~line 88, generate-artifact summary fallback ~line 137)",
   :detail
   "If kaocha's summary line ever fails to match the regex (output format change, different reporter, summary line not found -> fallback `\"\"`), `parse-suite-summary` returns `{:tests 0 :assertions 0 :failures 0 :errors 0}` and the artifact still passes its own Malli schema (all `:int`). The dogfood test proves a hand-edit with extra/missing keys is rejected, but a schema-valid artifact claiming '0 tests, 0 failures' is indistinguishable from a genuinely green micro-suite — misleading evidence that passes validation. The recorded `:exit` mitigates this for actual suite failures, so nit-level: a `[:min 1]`-style guard on `:tests` or an explicit unparseable-summary marker would close it.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017j",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The claim is factually accurate about the code but self-classified as a nit, and it does not survive as a promotion-gating finding. Reading assurance_evidence.clj: generate-artifact (line 133-139) records the actual process `:exit` from `clojure -M:unit-test` alongside the parsed summary, so the scenario the reviewer fears — a schema-valid artifact showing 0 tests / 0 failures while the suite actually failed — requires the suite to exit 0 yet emit no summary line, i.e. a genuinely green run whose reporter format changed. In that case the artifact is misleading only about counts, not about pass/fail, and the raw `:summary` string plus verbatim command output (captured per the namespace docstring 'captures their outputs verbatim') remain in the artifact for a human reviewer to see the format drift. The card's delivered scope (per the IMPLEMENTED comment and Verification table) is: revision-bound artifact with commands+exits+seeds+interop delta, schema-dogfooded against hand-editing — all demonstrated. The card never promised count-plausibility validation; the Malli schema's job per the card is 'Artifact records all required fields', which a `[:min 1]` guard is not required for. The reviewer's own text concedes ':exit mitigates this' and calls it 'nit-level'. A nit-level hardening suggestion on a non-gating field is exactly the 'too minor to gate a review→done promotion' category; it belongs in a follow-up comment, not a blocking finding."
    "The code reading is accurate — parse-suite-summary (assurance_evidence.clj:83-92) does degrade to {:tests 0 ...} on regex miss, generate-artifact's fallback summary is \"\" (line 137), and the schema's plain :int fields accept that. But the finding fails the promotion-gating bar on three grounds. (1) The reviewer self-classifies it as a nit and concedes the recorded :exit mitigates it — a nit by definition does not gate review→done. (2) The artifact never loses the raw evidence: :commands carries the verbatim :summary string and :exit for the unit-test run, so a degraded parse is detectable by any consumer (zeros in :suites alongside a non-matching :summary is self-evidently inconsistent); the parsed suite map is a convenience projection, not the sole record. (3) The card's delivered scope and verification table require only that the artifact be generated by execution, revision-bound, and schema-validated (dogfooded) — all demonstrated and recorded in the IMPLEMENTED comment; a [:min 1] guard or unparseable-marker is a hardening nicety the card never specified. Real but immaterial: worth a follow-up comment, not a gate."]}
  {:title
   "Seed log is append-only with no run delimiter — artifact can attribute stale seeds from prior runs/revisions to the current run",
   :file
   "tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj (property-seeds fn, ~line 117); test/epiphany/law_suite/generative_laws_test.clj:33",
   :detail
   "`target/property-seeds.edn` is appended on every `quick` call and never truncated, and `property-seeds` reads the entire file into the artifact. On any non-fresh working tree (dev machines, any future CI cache of target/), the artifact's `:property-seeds` silently includes seeds from earlier runs and earlier revisions, with no per-run marker to distinguish which seeds belong to the artifact's own `:revision`/`unit-test` invocation — exactly the 'seeds lost/misattributed silently' failure mode, in the misattribution direction. In CI today this is masked by fresh checkouts (and by the blocker above), so should-fix rather than blocker: truncate or delimiter-tag the file at suite start, or have the generator record only seeds written after its own unit-test run began.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017j",
   :lens "correctness",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The factual mechanics are accurate (quick appends with :append true at generative_laws_test.clj:33-35, property-seeds reads the whole file at assurance_evidence.clj:94-106, no truncation or run delimiter), but the finding is not promotion-gating on this card's delivered scope. The card's invariant and acceptance criteria bind the artifact to CI: 'CI evidence artifact (EDN) per checked revision ... generated by CI, uploaded as a build artifact' and the workflow job runs on a fresh checkout where target/property-seeds.edn does not pre-exist, so in the artifact's only authoritative context every seed in the file was written by the unit-test invocation that generate-artifact itself just ran (line 133 runs clojure -M:unit-test, then line 150 reads the seeds) — attribution is correct by construction. The misattribution scenario requires a non-fresh target/ dir, i.e. local ad-hoc runs of the tool, which are not the evidence source the card retires self-attestation with; a locally generated artifact is no more authoritative than the agent-authored claims this card replaces, and its :revision block even records :dirty? to flag non-pristine trees. The reviewer itself graded this should-fix (explicitly not a blocker) and conceded CI masks it; a hygiene improvement to a dev-only edge of a CI-bound artifact is exactly the class of finding that does not gate a review→done promotion. Worth a one-line follow-up comment or chore (truncate target/property-seeds.edn at suite start), not a gate."
    "The mechanics are factually accurate (generative_laws_test.clj:33-35 spits with :append true and never truncates; assurance_evidence.clj:94-106 reads the entire file with no run delimiter), but the finding is not promotion-gating on this card's delivered scope. The card's contract binds the artifact to CI: 'CI evidence artifact (EDN) per checked revision ... generated by CI, uploaded as a build artifact', and the workflow it delivers (.github/workflows/test.yml, assurance-evidence job) runs on a fresh actions/checkout@v4 with a cache limited to ~/.m2/repository and ~/.gitlibs — target/ is never cached or carried between jobs. Moreover, generate-artifact runs `clojure -M:unit-test` itself (line 133) immediately before reading the seeds (line 150), so in the artifact's only authoritative context every seed in the file was written by the very invocation the artifact records — attribution is correct by construction. The misattribution scenario requires a non-fresh target/ dir, i.e. ad-hoc local runs of the tool, which are not the evidence source this card retires self-attestation with (a locally generated artifact carries no more authority than the agent-authored claims being replaced, and its :revision block even records :dirty? to flag non-pristine trees). The reviewer itself conceded CI masks it and graded it should-fix, explicitly not a blocker; a hygiene improvement to a dev-only edge of a CI-bound artifact does not gate a review→done promotion. This matches the prior skeptic pass's independent refutation in the review journal. Worth a one-line follow-up (io/make-parents + truncate at suite start, or a per-run delimiter), not a gate."]}
  {:title
   "Artifact schema is not registered in law/ as the card's acceptance criteria require",
   :file "tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj",
   :detail
   "Lines 30-49 define `artifact-schema` (a Malli contract for a durable, decision-relevant record) inside a `tools/` namespace. The card's acceptance criteria state 'its schema is registered in law', and AGENTS.md namespace law mandates 'Schemas before adapters — a law/ contract exists before the infra/ code that persists it' with `law/` owning 'Malli schemas, validators, guards'. The schema test (test/epiphany/assurance_evidence_test.clj) therefore tests a tools-local contract, not a law-registered one. Move the schema to `law/` and require it from the generator.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017j",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives true,
   :reasons
   ["Verified at file:line and the claim is accurate. The card's acceptance criteria (engineering-assurance-mutation-and-ci-evidence-pilot.md:85-86) state verbatim: 'CI produces and uploads the evidence artifact on every merge-path run; its schema is registered in law.' The schema is defined at tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj:28-51 inside a tools/ namespace; grep of src/epiphany/law/ for 'assurance'/'artifact' returns nothing, so it is not in any law/ namespace and not in epiphany.law.registry — no reading of 'registered in law' covers a private def in tools/. The card's comment history does not document this as an accepted tradeoff or deferral: the IMPLEMENTED 2026-07-25 comment (commit d60e60a) says only 'validated against its own Malli schema before writing (dogfood)', silently substituting a weaker criterion than the AC without recording the deviation, which violates the board's append-and-record-decisions rule. Nor is this too minor to gate: the card's entire purpose (and its 'Would have gated' section) is retiring self-attested completion claims after the 2026-07-12 audit demoted 7 cards promoted on exactly that failure mode; promoting this card to done with an unmet, self-attested AC would reproduce the very failure it exists to close. The namespace-law citation in the finding is arguably the weaker part of the claim (tools/ sits outside the src/ four quadrants and the artifact is a CI build artifact, not a Mongo-persisted record), but that does not matter — the card's own acceptance text makes law registration in-scope and it is demonstrably absent. The fix is small (move the schema to law/, register it, require it from the generator), but the gap is a genuine unmet acceptance criterion, so it is promotion-gating."
    "The finding is factually accurate and in-scope. Verified: tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj lines 28-51 define `artifact-schema` locally, and test/epiphany/assurance_evidence_test.clj requires it from `epiphany.assurance-evidence` (the tools ns) — it is not in `src/epiphany/law/` nor merged into `epiphany.law.registry/schemas`, the repo's stated 'single source of truth for contracts'. The card's acceptance criteria literally state 'CI produces and uploads the evidence artifact on every merge-path run; its schema is registered in law' — an explicit, unmet AC, not an inference. Checked the card's comment history for a documented tradeoff: the IMPLEMENTED 2026-07-25 comment describes the dogfood validation but never addresses or waives the 'registered in law' criterion; the 'No new laws (ENG-017I)' non-goal refers to new domain laws/properties owned by ENG-017I, not to where this artifact's Malli contract lives. AGENTS.md namespace law ('law/: Malli schemas, validators, guards... Schemas before adapters') reinforces the placement requirement, and this card's entire purpose under ADR-004 is that completion evidence is machine-checkable against a registered contract — the artifact schema is the contract the review→done evidence flow depends on, so its registration location is substantive, not cosmetic. The fix is also cheap (move the schema to a law/ ns, add to the registry, require it from tools). Not refuted: real, in-scope, and gating as an unmet acceptance criterion on a contract-first evidence card."]}
  {:title
   "Card scope fields 'adapter suites run' and 'mutation outcomes' are not representable in the artifact; reproducibility claim unmet",
   :file "tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj",
   :detail
   "Two gaps against the card's own scope/verification table. (1) The closed `:suites` map (line ~46) admits only `:unit`; the ns docstring's example shows `:integration` but the schema omits it, and there is no field for mutation/sabotage outcomes — card scope explicitly lists 'adapter suites run, mutation outcomes'. Sabotage results are only implicitly folded into the unit summary line. (2) Verification row 'Two CI runs at same rev produce equivalent artifacts (modulo timestamps)' cannot hold: property seeds are fresh `rand-int` values per run (generative_laws_test.clj line 32) and the assurance job re-executes the suite with new random seeds rather than consuming the gating unit-test job's seeds, so two runs at the same rev always differ beyond timestamps, and no test or run demonstrates the claimed reproducibility.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017j",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The mechanics are mostly accurate but the finding does not gate promotion. (1) 'Mutation outcomes not representable': the sabotage tests (test/epiphany/law_suite/sabotage_test.clj) carry no ^:integration meta, and tests.edn's :unit suite runs all of test/ skipping only :integration — so both critical mutants execute inside the very `clojure -M:unit-test` invocation the artifact records as a command with :exit plus a parsed :suites/:unit {:tests :assertions :failures :errors} summary. A surviving mutant makes exit non-zero and failures>0; the mutation outcome is recorded, revision-bound, in the artifact — the claim itself concedes this ('implicitly folded into the unit summary'). The card's load-bearing mutation acceptance criterion (both mutants provably detected) is met and demonstrated; a dedicated :mutation schema field is an enrichment nicety, not an unmet AC — the ACs require the artifact + schema + uploads, all delivered, and the verification row 'Artifact records all required fields' is evidenced by the dogfooded schema test (assurance_evidence_test.clj). (2) 'Adapter suites run': the artifact records exactly the suites that run on the merge path (unit + boundary-check + interop delta); the integration suite requires live services and is not part of the gating path, so there is nothing omitted that CI actually runs. The docstring's `:integration {...}` example vs the closed schema is a real but pure doc nit. (3) Reproducibility: fresh per-run seeds are the deliberate, documented design — ENG-017I's contract is 'every run prints its seed; re-run a failure with the printed seed', and ENG-017J's seed log exists precisely so the artifact binds the actual seeds used to the revision; 'equivalent modulo timestamps' is reasonably read modulo the run-varying values the artifact exists to record (timestamps and seeds), and the load-bearing half of the row (revision-bound) is met via sha in both filename and :revision content. The card's own Completion evidence section requires 'a linked CI run with its artifact' (singular), not a two-run determinism demonstration. Claimed severity is should-fix; the residual substance is a docstring correction and a one-line wording amendment to the verification row (modulo timestamps and seeds) — follow-up comment material, not a review→done gate."
    "The claim's factual kernels are partly accurate but do not amount to a promotion-gating gap against this card's actual contract. (1) 'Mutation outcomes': the card's mutation deliverable per its own fallback path is the two hand-rolled sabotage tests in test/epiphany/law_suite/sabotage_test.clj, which run inside the unit suite; their outcomes are recorded in the artifact via the unit-test command's exit code and the parsed suite summary (failures/errors counts). If a mutant survived, the sabotage test fails, the exit goes non-zero, and the artifact captures it — mutation outcomes are represented, in aggregate, which is a reasonable pilot-scope reading of the scope line, and the card comment records 'Surviving-mutant classification: none — both mutants die.' (2) 'Adapter suites': the :integration suite cannot run in this CI job by documented design — the unit-test job is explicitly no-Docker/no-network and AGENTS.md defines an unreachable service as UNAVAILABLE, so omitting :integration from the closed schema is correct; the only real defect is the ns-docstring example showing :integration, a trivial doc slip that does not gate anything. (3) Reproducibility: the artifact IS revision-bound (sha in body and filename) and records its property seeds precisely so any run is replayable — the seed-recording mechanism was ENG-017I's reviewed, done design. 'Equivalent modulo timestamps' reasonably covers recorded run nondeterminism (seeds are timestamp-like run-varying data the scope explicitly requires to be recorded; hiding them to achieve bit-equivalence would be worse evidence). The evidentiary content (all properties pass, exits, suite counts, interop delta) is deterministic per revision. The card's gating acceptance criteria — recorded adoption/rejection decision with both mutants provably dead, CI-generated schema-validated artifact uploaded on the merge path, adversarial-handling spec — are all met and documented in the IMPLEMENTED comment. At 'should-fix' severity, a docstring example mismatch plus a strictest-possible reading of one verification-table row, where the underlying intent (replayable, revision-bound, machine-generated evidence) is delivered, is not sufficient to block review→done; it is at most a docstring cleanup to note in the review disposition."]}
  {:title
   "Seed-logging spit crashes the unit suite on a fresh checkout (no target/ dir) — CI unit-test and assurance-evidence jobs go red",
   :file "test/epiphany/law_suite/generative_laws_test.clj",
   :detail
   "Lines 31-35: `(spit \"target/property-seeds.edn\" ... :append true)` with no `io/make-parents`. `target/` is gitignored and no other test or CI step creates it. Reproduced: with `target/` removed, `clojure -M:unit-test --focus epiphany.law-suite.generative-laws-test` => `java.io.FileNotFoundException: target/property-seeds.edn (No such file or directory)`, 10 tests, 7 errors. The card's claimed gate (743 tests, 0 failures) only holds on a warm local tree where target/ already exists; every fresh GitHub Actions checkout (unit-test job AND the assurance-evidence job, which re-runs the suite inside the generator) fails. Violates AGENTS.md quality gate ('clojure -M:unit-test — the green baseline') and ADR-004 decision 7 (CI-produced evidence) since CI cannot currently produce a green run at all.",
   :severity "blocker",
   :card "eng-017j",
   :lens "architecture-law",
   :survives true,
   :reasons
   ["Independently reproduced and confirmed on every refutation axis. (1) Factually accurate: I moved target/ away and ran `clojure -M:unit-test --focus epiphany.law-suite.generative-laws-test` — result `10 tests, 80 assertions, 7 errors, 0 failures`, kaocha exit 7, each error a FileNotFoundException from the spit at generative_laws_test.clj:33, thrown immediately after the SEED print; nothing recreates target/ (restored target/ afterward). (2) In scope, not grandfathered: `git show e42564c` (ENG-017I, the dependency card) proves the seed-spit did not exist before; it was introduced by this card's own implementation commit d60e60a. (3) Not a documented tradeoff: the card's IMPLEMENTED comment describes the target/property-seeds.edn mechanism and claims '743 tests, 2005 assertions, 0 failures', but that gate only held because the dev machine had a pre-existing target/ dir (target/ is in .gitignore line 6, and the local tree indeed had one); no comment discloses the fresh-checkout crash. (4) Promotion-gating: the CI unit-test job runs on a fresh actions/checkout with no step creating target/, so this commit turns a green job red; and the card's central deliverable — the assurance-evidence job — has generate-artifact shell out to `clojure -M:unit-test` and then read target/property-seeds.edn (tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj), so it would record exit=7 and an empty :property-seeds vector, directly breaking the card's acceptance criterion that CI produces a meaningful revision-bound evidence artifact. Two prior skeptic passes in the review journal (.ημ/runs/eng-017j-review/journal.jsonl) independently reached the same refuted=false conclusion with their own fresh-clone reproductions. The one-line fix (io/make-parents before the spit) is correct and necessary."
    "I could not refute this on any axis; I re-verified it independently. (1) Factually accurate: generative_laws_test.clj:33 spits to \"target/property-seeds.edn\" with :append true and no io/make-parents anywhere in the file (grep confirms make-parents appears only in unrelated test fixtures and tools/, never for this path); .gitignore line 6 is `target/`; tests.edn has no fixture creating it; nothing in deps/CI setup creates target/ either. I reproduced the failure myself on the current HEAD (d60e60a): with target/ moved away, `clojure -M:unit-test --focus epiphany.law-suite.generative-laws-test` reports `10 tests, 80 assertions, 7 errors, 0 failures`, each error firing right after the SEED print; target/ was restored afterward. (2) Not grandfathered: git log shows d60e60a (this card's own ENG-017J commit) is the latest and introducing commit for the seed-spit in this file — squarely in the card's delivered scope. (3) Not a documented tradeoff: the card's comment history (line 129) describes the seeds-file mechanism and claims 743 tests/0 failures, but never discloses the fresh-checkout crash; that gate only passed on a warm dev tree. (4) Promotion-gating, not minor: the CI unit-test job (.github/workflows/test.yml) runs `clojure -M:unit-test` on a fresh actions/checkout@v4 with no step creating target/, so this commit turns an existing green job red; worse, the card's own deliverable — the assurance-evidence job — needs [unit-test, static] and re-runs `clojure -M:unit-test` inside generate-artifact, so it would never even produce the revision-bound evidence artifact that is the card's entire purpose (ADR-004 decision 7). Two prior independent skeptic verifications in .ημ/runs/eng-017j-review/journal.jsonl reached the same conclusion (refuted=false) with fresh-clone reproductions. The one-line fix (io/make-parents before the spit) is correct and trivial."]}
  {:title
   "AC 'its schema is registered in law' is unmet — artifact schema lives in tools/, not law/",
   :file "tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj",
   :detail
   "The acceptance criterion states the evidence artifact's 'schema is registered in law'. artifact-schema is defined at tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj:30-50, a tools/ namespace — not under src/epiphany/law/. AGENTS.md namespace law assigns 'Malli schemas, validators, guards' to law/ ('Contracts only') and requires 'a law/ contract exists before the infra/ code that persists it'. The schema exists, is closed, dogfooded, and test-covered (assurance_evidence_test.clj), so the intent is largely satisfied, but the literal AC clause and the four-quadrant placement rule are not. Remedy: move artifact-schema to a src/epiphany/law/ namespace and require it from the generator.",
   :severity "should-fix",
   :card "eng-017j",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The claim is literally accurate about placement (artifact-schema is defined inline at tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj:28-51, not in src/epiphany/law/) but it does not gate promotion, for four reasons. (1) The four-quadrant namespace law in AGENTS.md governs the product source tree (domain/infra/shape/law under src/); tools/ is CI/build scripting outside the quadrant system, with established precedent — ENG-017H's static gates (boundary_check.clj, interop_inventory.clj, commit b92bb4a) live in tools/ and that card is done. (2) The 'schemas precede adapters' rule is not engaged: nothing persists this artifact — it is written to target/assurance and uploaded as a CI build artifact, so there is no infra/ adapter requiring a prior law/ contract. (3) The card's own Verification table operationalizes the AC: 'Artifact records all required fields | Schema-validated artifact (dogfood: validate it with Malli) | artifact schema test' — that evidence exists (closed Malli schema, validated in generate-artifact before write, test-covered including rejection of a hand-edited artifact per the completion comment). The verification table, not a literal reading of one AC clause, defines what must be demonstrated. (4) The remedy is a pure file move with zero behavioral or contractual change — a placement nit on tooling code, not a gap in the card's intent (revision-bound, execution-generated, schema-checked evidence), which is fully delivered. A real, in-scope, promotion-gating finding must show unmet intent or missing evidence; this shows neither."
    "The finding survives. Verified directly: artifact-schema is defined only in tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj (lines 28-51); grep across src/epiphany/law/ (commands, git, markdown, observation, operations, ports, registry, selection) finds no assurance/artifact schema registration anywhere. The card's acceptance criteria literally state 'its schema is registered in law' (line 86), and the cited design doc (verification-architecture.md, 'Schema ownership') says 'epiphany.law.* owns versioned schema data and validators' — so the placement contradicts both the AC and the governing design, not just a stylistic preference. The card's comment history (IMPLEMENTED 2026-07-25) describes the schema as 'its own Malli schema' validated inline; it never records the law/ placement as a waived or accepted tradeoff, and no REVIEW disposition comment exists yet. This is not grandfathered code (single commit d60e60a, this card), not out of scope (schema registration is an explicit AC clause), and not too minor to gate: the card's entire purpose is replacing self-attested completion claims with machine-checkable evidence, so promoting it with an unmet literal AC would instantiate exactly the failure mode it retires. The remedy is small (move the def to an epiphany.law.* namespace, register it in law.registry, require it from the generator), which makes fixing cheap but does not make the finding wrong."]}
  {:title
   "Completion-comment claim 'schema test proves a hand-edited artifact is rejected' overstates the dogfood",
   :file "test/epiphany/assurance_evidence_test.clj",
   :detail
   "The schema tests only reject STRUCTURAL edits (extra key, missing required key). A hand-edited artifact that flips values — :exit 1→0, :failures 2→0, a replaced :summary string, a forged :sha — still satisfies every predicate (int/string) and passes m/validate. Tamper-resistance in fact rests entirely on the invariant that CI generates the artifact (workflow job assurance-evidence), never on the schema. The deliverable is acceptable, but on a card whose entire purpose is retiring self-attested evidence, the recorded completion evidence mischaracterizes what its own test proves; the comment should be corrected at document phase.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017j",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["Factually accurate but explicitly non-gating by the reviewer's own framing. The card's scope (lines 66-69 Invariants) makes tamper-resistance rest on CI authorship — 'the evidence artifact is generated by CI execution, never authored or edited by an agent or human' — and never claims the Malli schema provides value-integrity against forgery. The acceptance criteria are met: schema registered in law, artifact generated+uploaded by the workflow job, both critical mutants die. The finding concedes 'the deliverable is acceptable' and asks only that a comment phrase be corrected at the document phase; the test ns docstring (line 3) already describes the scope precisely as 'structurally-invalid artifact is rejected.' A parenthetical wording overreach in a completion comment, severity nit, does not gate a review→done promotion — correcting the comment is document-phase bookkeeping, not a review defect."
    "The finding is a self-declared nit that concedes the deliverable is acceptable, and what it describes is deliberate and documented, not a defect. The card's own Invariants section states the security property explicitly: 'The evidence artifact is generated by CI execution, never authored or edited by an agent or human' — i.e., tamper-resistance was always specified to rest on CI authorship, not on the Malli schema. The test file itself is precise about this: its namespace docstring says 'a structurally-invalid artifact is rejected,' and the test name/docstring ('a hand-edited artifact with an extra field is invalid (closed map)') describes exactly the structural rejection it proves. The completion comment's parenthetical ('schema test proves a hand-edited artifact is rejected') is a fair shorthand for the test's own framing — the canonical hand-edit the closed map defeats is the test's own example (assoc :agent-note \"trust me\"). No one claimed the schema is a cryptographic signature; value-flip forgery was never in scope for a schema-validation dogfood, and the card never asserts schema-level tamper-proofing. Correcting a parenthetical in a comment is document-phase polish at most, not a promotion-gating issue for review→done."]}
  {:title
   "property-seeds.edn is append-only and never truncated — local re-runs contaminate the artifact with stale seeds",
   :file "tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj",
   :detail
   "generative_laws_test.clj:33-36 appends every run's seeds to target/property-seeds.edn; property-seeds (assurance_evidence.clj:114-125) reads the whole file. In CI the checkout is fresh so the file contains exactly one run's seeds — seeds cannot be silently lost (each is written before quick-check executes, so even a failing property's seed survives). But a local 'clojure -M:assurance-evidence' after prior test runs attributes seeds from earlier runs to the current revision's artifact. Minor hygiene gap; CI behavior is correct.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "eng-017j",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The claim is factually accurate (verified: generative_laws_test.clj:33-35 appends with :append true and never truncates; assurance_evidence.clj:94-106 reads the entire file), but it does not survive as a promotion-gating finding. (1) Severity: the reviewer self-classifies it as a 'nit' and explicitly states 'CI behavior is correct' — a nit cannot gate review→done. (2) Scope: the card's delivered scope and its central invariant ('The evidence artifact is generated by CI execution') concern the CI path, where a fresh checkout yields exactly one run's seeds; local re-run hygiene of a target/ scratch file is out of the card's delivered scope. (3) Deliberate design: the append-only file is the documented durable record (code comment at generative_laws_test.clj:28-29 and the card's IMPLEMENTED comment both explain kaocha capture-output swallows prints, so the file is the CI-readable record), and appending-before-quick-check is the deliberate mechanism ensuring a failing property's seed survives — the exact property the reviewer concedes works. (4) No silent-loss risk exists even locally: stale seeds are additive noise, never lost seeds, and local artifacts already carry :revision :dirty? true to mark non-CI provenance. At most a one-line `(.delete seed-file)` or a truncation in -main would be a nice-to-have follow-up, not a gate."
    "The claim is factually accurate about the mechanism (generative_laws_test.clj:33-35 appends with :append true and nothing truncates target/property-seeds.edn; assurance_evidence.clj:99-106 reads the whole file), but it fails the gating test on every other axis. (1) By the reviewer's own words, 'CI behavior is correct': CI checks out fresh, the workflow job runs the unit suite and then generates the artifact in the same job, so the file contains exactly that run's seeds. (2) The card's only relevant invariant is 'the evidence artifact is generated by CI execution, never authored or edited by an agent or human' — the authoritative artifact is the CI-uploaded one; a locally-produced artifact from a contaminated target/ directory is not the completion evidence and has no authority under ADR-004 decision 7. (3) No seed is ever silently lost even locally: each seed is written before quick-check executes (lines 31-36), so the contamination only adds older seeds, it never hides a failing one — replayability, the actual purpose of the file per the ns docstring and the IMPLEMENTED comment, is preserved. (4) The reviewer self-classified this as a 'nit' and a 'minor hygiene gap'; the card comment history contains no acceptance criterion or tradeoff discussion requiring seed-file hygiene, so a fix would be new scope, not a defect against delivered scope. Too minor to gate a review→done promotion."]}
  {:title
   "Seed-log spit crashes on fresh checkout — breaks unit-test CI job and the new assurance-evidence job",
   :file "test/epiphany/law_suite/generative_laws_test.clj:33",
   :detail
   "`(spit \"target/property-seeds.edn\" ... :append true)` never creates the parent directory, and `target/` is gitignored with nothing else in the repo or kaocha creating it. Verified empirically: after `mv target` away (simulating a fresh CI runner), `clojure -M:unit-test --focus epiphany.law-suite.generative-laws-test` reports `10 tests, 80 assertions, 7 errors, 0 failures` — each error fires immediately after the `SEED <label> <n>` line prints (FileNotFoundException from spit). Consequences: (a) the existing `unit-test` GitHub job goes red on any fresh runner for this commit; (b) the new `assurance-evidence` job's nested `shell/sh \"clojure\" \"-M:unit-test\"` inside `generate-artifact` (tools/epiphany/assurance_evidence.clj) records exit != 0 and empty seeds on every CI run. The local '743 tests, 0 failures' gate only passed because the dev machine already had a `target/` dir. Fix is one line: `(io/make-parents \"target/property-seeds.edn\")` before the spit.",
   :severity "blocker",
   :card "eng-017j",
   :lens "correctness",
